  "image_name": "5655c6453e58c74c9121bc826307549e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how voltage is generated and lost in conventional crystalline silicon and organic solar cells, while also showing the organic device structure. On the left, a bulk heterojunction organic cell is sketched between an anode/HTL and ETL/cathode. Intermixed donor and acceptor domains form a maze-like morphology. A light-created exciton appears near a donor–acceptor interface, where it separates into a hole and an electron that then travel through different pathways toward opposite electrodes.\n\nThe middle and right panels summarize the energetics. For a c-Si solar cell, absorbed energy is represented by the semiconductor bandgap between valence and conduction bands. The open-circuit voltage is lower than this absorbed energy because of a total voltage loss, split into radiative and non-radiative parts. In the organic solar cell, the absorbed optical gap is shown between ground and excited singlet states, but the output voltage is tied to a lower interfacial charge-transfer state, labeled ECT. This highlights an extra energetic offset between absorption and charge separation, followed again by radiative and non-radiative voltage losses. Overall, the figure emphasizes why organic solar cells typically lose more voltage than inorganic ones.",
